---
/* -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
   PROTOTYPE — condensed consultation page, booking in the hero.

   A variant of /consultation/, not a replacement: nothing links here and the
   page is noindex, so the two can be reviewed side by side. Fold it into
   consultation.astro (or delete it) once a direction is picked.

   The shipped page answers "what is this?" thoroughly and then asks — hero,
   proof, three deliverable cards, the marketing panel, and only then the
   scheduler at roughly half the page. That ordering is why every CTA used to
   deep-link past it. This variant tests the opposite bet: put the calendar in
   the hero, next to the headline, and let the explanation ride alongside it in
   three lines rather than three screens.

   What comes out to make room:
   - the diagnostic panel (website / social / content) — the calendar takes its
     column
   - the "reach is an amplifier" marketing panel — the longest block between
     landing and booking, and it argues a point nobody has asked yet
   - the standalone booking section and its trust row — folded into the hero as
     chips
   What stays, in this order: hero+panel, proof, what you walk away with. The
   deliverable cards move BELOW the ask, where they catch the person who
   scrolled instead of booking rather than delaying the one who would have.

   Both paths survive the condensing, and neither is the consolation prize:
   the panel is a two-state widget with "Pick a time" and "Send a Message" as
   equal halves of a switch, swapping the pane in place. Nobody who wants the
   form has to scroll past the calendar to reach it, and there is no second
   copy of the form further down the page.

   Analytics locations all carry a `_condensed` suffix so this page and the
   shipped one are separable in Plausible; that comparison is the entire point
   of keeping both alive.
--------------------------------------------------------------------------- */
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import LeadForm from '../components/LeadForm.astro';
import { getDoc, getProjects } from '../lib/sanity.js';

const c = await getDoc('consultationPage');
const settings = await getDoc('siteSettings');
const projects = await getProjects();

const SCHEDULE_URL = settings.scheduleUrl;
const CAL_EMBED_URL = settings.calEmbedUrl;

const description =
  'The Pulse Check: a free 30-minute review for organizations at any stage. Walk away with an honest read on where you stand and a one-page written readout within 48 hours.';

// Condensed to three lines from the strip on /consultation/. Same promises,
// same source of truth (pulse-check-questionnaire.md) — this is the version
// that has to sit beside a calendar without becoming a wall of text.
const HOW = [
  { when: 'Before', body: 'Nothing to prepare. We look at what you have first.' },
  { when: '30 minutes', body: 'Can they find you, trust you, choose you.' },
  { when: 'After', body: 'A one-page readout within 48 hours. Yours to keep.' },
];
---
<Base
  title="Free 30-Minute Consultation (condensed) — Panta"
  description={description}
  noindex
>
  <!-- HERO + BOOKING -->
  <section class="section section--dark hero" id="book" data-section="condensed_hero">
    <div class="container hero__grid">
      {/* Three blocks, not two, so the calendar can sit between them on narrow
          screens: headline → calendar → detail. Stacking the whole left column
          first pushed the embed to ~850px on a 375 viewport, a full screen
          below the fold, which is the thing this variant exists to avoid. */}
      <div class="hero__intro">
        <p class="label">{c.heroLabel}</p>
        <h1 class="hero__title">{c.heroTitle}</h1>
        <p class="lede hero__lede">{c.heroLede}</p>
      </div>

      <div class="hero__detail">
        <ul class="how">
          {HOW.map((step) => (
            <li class="how__step">
              <span class="how__when">{step.when}</span>
              <span class="how__body">{step.body}</span>
            </li>
          ))}
        </ul>

      </div>

      {/* The panel holds both paths and shows one at a time. "Send a Message" was
          a text link under the copy, which made the calendar the offer and the
          form a consolation; as an equal half of a switch it is the same size
          ask, and choosing it swaps the pane in place rather than scrolling
          somewhere else — the visitor who did not want the calendar never has
          to travel past it.

          The switch is two radios and two labels, no JavaScript: with a script
          blocked or still loading it still works, which a button-and-JS tab
          would not. Tracking rides on data-track attributes on the labels, so
          the delegated listener in Analytics.astro reports the split without
          this page owning any of it.

          The iframe is not loading="lazy": it is above the fold and it is the
          default pane, so deferring it would only delay the one thing this
          variant exists to reach sooner. A third-party iframe in the hero does
          cost LCP — that trade is the hypothesis under test. */}
      <div class="panel">
        <input class="panel__radio" type="radio" name="panel-view" id="view-calendar" checked />
        <input class="panel__radio" type="radio" name="panel-view" id="view-note" />

        <div class="panel__switch">
          <label
            class="panel__tab"
            for="view-calendar"
            data-track="cta_click"
            data-track-cta="choose_calendar"
            data-track-location="condensed_panel"
          >Pick a time</label>
          <label
            class="panel__tab"
            for="view-note"
            data-track="cta_click"
            data-track-cta="choose_note"
            data-track-location="condensed_panel"
          >Send a Message</label>
        </div>

        <div class="panel__body">
          <div class="panel__pane pane--calendar">
            <div class="book-embed">
              <iframe
                src={CAL_EMBED_URL}
                title="Book your free consultation with Panta"
                width="100%"
                height="640"></iframe>
            </div>
            <p class="panel__fallback">
              Calendar not loading?
              <a
                href={SCHEDULE_URL}
                target="_blank"
                rel="noopener noreferrer"
                data-track="cta_click"
                data-track-cta="booking_new_tab"
                data-track-location="condensed_booking"
              >Open it in a new tab &rarr;</a>
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="panel__pane pane--note">
            <p class="panel__intro">
              Tell us what brings you to us. We reply within one business day.
            </p>
            {/* bookHref is in-page: this panel holds the scheduler, so someone
                who just sent a note should get the calendar back, not a trip to
                /consultation/. A fragment cannot check a radio on its own, so
                the script below does it — which is always available here,
                because without JS the form posts and /api/contact redirects to
                /contact/?sent=1 and this success panel never renders at all. */}
            <LeadForm source="condensed_panel" bookHref="#view-calendar" />
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="container">
      <div class="trust-row">
        {c.trustRow.map((item) => (
          <span><span class="ck">&#10003;</span> {item}</span>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- PROOF -->
  <div class="proof">
    <div class="container proof__inner">
      <span class="proof__q">{c.proofLabel}</span>
      {projects.map((p) => <span class="client">{p.name}</span>)}
    </div>
  </div>

  <!-- WHAT YOU WALK AWAY WITH — reinforcement, not preamble -->
  <section class="section" data-section="condensed_deliver">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="label">{c.deliverLabel}</p>
      <h2>{c.deliverTitle}</h2>
      <p>{c.deliverIntro}</p>
      <div class="grid grid--3" style="margin-top: 2.5rem;">
        {c.deliverCards.map((card) => (
          <div class="card">
            <p class="card__num">{card.kicker}</p>
            <h3>{card.title}</h3>
            <p>{card.body}</p>
          </div>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {/* No standalone message section: the form is a pane of the hero panel
      now, and a second copy further down would be the same request asked
      twice, with two sets of field ids and two sources muddying the split. */}
</Base>

<script>
  // Same caveat as consultation.astro: the Google Calendar embed is
  // cross-origin and reports no completion event, so a booking is not
  // observable from here. What IS newly worth measuring on this variant is
  // that the calendar starts in view — `booking_viewed` firing on load rather
  // than after a scroll is the difference between the two pages.
  const track = (window as any).pantaTrack ?? (() => {});
  const embed = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('.book-embed');

  if (embed) {
    const observer = new IntersectionObserver(
      (entries) => {
        for (const entry of entries) {
          if (!entry.isIntersecting) continue;
          track('booking_viewed', { variant: 'condensed' });
          observer.disconnect();
        }
      },
      { threshold: 0.4 }
    );
    observer.observe(embed);
  }

  // "Rather just pick a time?" in the message pane success panel. Its href is
  // the radio's id, which a fragment jump cannot check on its own.
  const calendarView = document.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('#view-calendar');
  document.querySelector('.panel')?.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
    const link = (e.target as Element)?.closest?.('a[href="#view-calendar"]');
    if (!link || !calendarView) return;
    e.preventDefault();
    calendarView.checked = true;
    track('cta_click', { cta: 'choose_calendar', location: 'condensed_panel_after_send' });
  });
</script>

<style>
  .hero { padding-block: clamp(2.5rem, 5vw, 3.5rem); }

  .hero__grid {
    display: grid;
    /* The calendar gets the wider column: Google's appointment embed goes
       cramped much under ~520px. Measured at 1280 this split puts the iframe
       itself (inside .panel's padding) at ~530. */
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.72fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    grid-template-areas:
      'intro panel'
      'detail panel';
    gap: 0 clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3.5rem);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .hero__intro { grid-area: intro; }
  .panel { grid-area: panel; }
  .hero__detail { grid-area: detail; }

  /* One column, and the calendar jumps the queue: headline, then book, then
     the detail for anyone still reading. */
  @media (max-width: 940px) {
    .hero__grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      grid-template-areas:
        'intro'
        'panel'
        'detail';
      gap: 1.75rem 0;
    }
  }

  .hero__title {
    font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3rem);
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .hero__lede { margin-bottom: 1.5rem; }

  /* how it goes — three lines, not three screens */
  .how {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0 0 1.5rem;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    gap: 0.7rem;
  }

  .how__step {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 6.5rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: 0.9rem;
    align-items: baseline;
    padding-top: 0.7rem;
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(245, 241, 231, 0.18);
  }

  @media (max-width: 460px) {
    .how__step { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: 0.2rem; }
  }

  .how__when {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: 0.62rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--sage);
  }

  .how__body {
    font-size: 0.92rem;
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--cream);
  }

  /* the panel — the cream readout the diagnostic panel used to be, now
     holding both paths behind a switch */
  .panel {
    background: var(--cream);
    color: var(--charcoal);
    border-radius: 10px;
    padding: 0.85rem;
    box-shadow: 0 24px 60px -30px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.55);
  }

  /* Visually hidden, not display:none — the radios stay focusable and stay in
     the tab order, which is the whole accessibility story for this switch. */
  .panel__radio {
    position: absolute;
    width: 1px;
    height: 1px;
    overflow: hidden;
    clip: rect(0 0 0 0);
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .panel__switch {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 4px;
    padding: 4px;
    margin-bottom: 0.85rem;
    background: var(--cream-soft);
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 999px;
  }

  .panel__tab {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    padding: 0.6rem 0.75rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--stone-deep);
    cursor: pointer;
    user-select: none;
    transition: background 0.15s ease, color 0.15s ease;
  }

  .panel__tab:hover { color: var(--charcoal); }

  #view-calendar:checked ~ .panel__switch label[for='view-calendar'],
  #view-note:checked ~ .panel__switch label[for='view-note'] {
    background: var(--charcoal);
    color: var(--cream);
  }

  #view-calendar:focus-visible ~ .panel__switch label[for='view-calendar'],
  #view-note:focus-visible ~ .panel__switch label[for='view-note'] {
    outline: 2px solid var(--bamboo-deep);
    outline-offset: 2px;
  }

  .panel__pane { display: none; }

  #view-calendar:checked ~ .panel__body .pane--calendar,
  #view-note:checked ~ .panel__body .pane--note {
    display: block;
  }

  .panel__intro {
    font-size: 0.87rem;
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: var(--stone-deep);
    margin: 0 0 1.1rem;
  }

  /* No min-height on .panel__body. Pinning it to the calendar's 674px stopped
     the swap reflowing, but left 238px of blank cream under the submit button,
     which reads as a broken pane rather than a stable one. The panes size to
     their own content instead: on desktop the hero's height is set by the copy
     column anyway, so the section barely moves, and a card resizing in
     response to a tab the visitor just clicked is expected behaviour. */

  .book-embed {
    background: #fffdf7;
    border: 1px solid var(--line);
    border-radius: 8px;
    overflow: hidden;
    scroll-margin-top: 2rem;
  }

  .book-embed iframe {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    min-height: 640px;
    border: 0;
  }

  .panel__fallback {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    color: var(--stone-deep);
    margin: 0.7rem 0 0;
  }

  .trust-row {
    display: flex;
    gap: clamp(1rem, 3vw, 1.75rem);
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    margin-top: 2rem;
    padding-top: 1.5rem;
    border-top: 1px solid rgba(245, 241, 231, 0.18);
  }

  .trust-row span {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--cream);
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 7px;
  }

  .trust-row .ck {
    color: var(--sage);
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  /* proof strip */
  .proof {
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);
    background: var(--cream-soft);
  }

  .proof__inner {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    gap: clamp(1.25rem, 4vw, 2.5rem);
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    padding-block: 1.25rem;
  }

  .proof__q {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--stone-deep);
  }

  .client {
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    color: var(--charcoal);
    opacity: 0.85;
  }

  .card__num {
    font-size: 0.72rem;
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    color: var(--bamboo-deep);
    margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
  }

  /* --- the form on a light island inside a dark section --------------------
     The panel is cream but it lives inside .section--dark, so the dark-surface
     rules in LeadForm.astro and global.css both fire on it: fields became
     cream text in a 8%-cream box, and .btn--filled inverted to a cream button.
     All of it cream on cream — the fields were invisible.

     The inner half is :global because .lead and .btn--filled belong to
     LeadForm's scope and global.css, not this page's.

     Two ancestors, not one, and that is load-bearing. Astro stamps its scope
     attribute on every non-global element in a selector, so LeadForm's own
     rule compiles to `.section--dark .lead[cid] input[cid]` — 0,4,1, one class
     MORE than the obvious `.pane--note :global(.lead input)` at 0,3,1, which
     is why the first attempt at this changed nothing. Prefixing .panel__body
     brings it to 0,5,1 and it wins outright rather than by source order. */
  .panel__body .pane--note :global(.lead input),
  .panel__body .pane--note :global(.lead textarea) {
    background: #fffdf7;
    border-color: var(--line);
    color: var(--charcoal);
  }

  .panel__body .pane--note :global(.lead input::placeholder),
  .panel__body .pane--note :global(.lead textarea::placeholder) {
    color: var(--stone-deep);
  }

  .panel__body .pane--note :global(.lead input:focus),
  .panel__body .pane--note :global(.lead textarea:focus) {
    outline-color: var(--bamboo-deep);
    border-color: var(--bamboo-deep);
  }

  .panel__body .pane--note :global(.lead__msg[data-state='ok']) { color: var(--bamboo-deep); }
  .panel__body .pane--note :global(.lead__msg[data-state='error']) { color: #8a4a2f; }

  /* Back to the base .btn--filled: charcoal fill, bamboo on hover. This one is
     unscoped in global.css, so it needs less reach than the rules above. */
  .panel__body .pane--note :global(.btn--filled) {
    background: var(--charcoal);
    color: var(--cream);
    border-color: var(--charcoal);
  }

  .panel__body .pane--note :global(.btn--filled:hover) {
    background: var(--bamboo-deep);
    border-color: var(--bamboo-deep);
    color: var(--cream);
  }
</style>
